17:30:43	./sim-root/runs/r12/edgeops	update edgeops-011 --notes Would run against the fleet, in order (none run here):
1. Capacity check on us-west-2 (edgeops-mol-h7z).
2. ops/drain.sh <us-west-2 node id>  (edgectl pool remove, then edgectl wait-drain --timeout 300).
3. cd terraform && terraform plan -out=patch-us-west-2.tfplan -var='image_overrides={"us-west-2"="<patched image id>"}' -replace='edge_node.fleet["us-west-2"]'   (needs terraform 1.6 or newer; this machine has 1.5.7).
4. Read the plan: only edge_node.fleet["us-west-2"] is replaced.
5. terraform apply patch-us-west-2.tfplan, then delete the plan file.
6. Confirm the new node runs the patched kernel and is healthy (edgectl subcommand to confirm first).
7. Return the node to the pool (edgectl pool add: not in the repo, confirm the subcommand first).
8. Watch the error rate for fifteen minutes before any other region has maintenance.
If verify fails: do not return the node to the pool. Remove us-west-2 from image_overrides and replace again.
After this region is verified: set var.image to the patched image, roll the other regions one at a time, then clear image_overrides.

Gotchas found: README.md says eleven nodes in four regions, but terraform declares one node per region. The local terraform is 1.5.7 and main.tf requires 1.6 or newer. dotdiw check persists formula templates, and dotdiw ready lists their unblocked steps (mol-drain-node.capacity); ignore those.
